-- title:
--   Hecke independence over ℂ from a period package
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \geq 1$ and an integer $k$, and write $S_k =$ `CuspForm (CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0 N) k`. Let $V$ be a complex vector space, $X$ a type, $ev : V \to (X \to \mathbb{C})$ an injective $\mathbb{C}$-linear coordinate map, $P : S_k \to V$ an injective $\mathbb{C}$-linear map, $\sigma$ a $\mathbb{C}$-linear endomorphism of $V$, and $L$ an additive subgroup of $V$, subject to four hypotheses: (a) for every $v \in L$ and every $x \in X$ the coordinate $ev(v)(x)$ is a rational number; (b) $P(f)$ lies in the $\mathbb{C}$-span of $L$ for every $f \in S_k$; (c) every $v \in L$ can be written $v = P(f) + \sigma(P(g))$ with $f, g \in S_k$; (d) every $t$ in [`CuspForm.heckeGenerators N k ∅`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L14), that is every endomorphism of $S_k$ of the form `heckeTLin k hℓ hℓN` for a prime $\ell \nmid N$ or `heckeULin k hqN` for a prime $q \mid N$, admits a transported operator: a $\mathbb{C}$-linear $A : V \to V$ with $P(t f) = A(P f)$ for all $f \in S_k$, with $A \circ \sigma = \sigma \circ A$, and with $A(L) \subseteq L$. Then for any family $T : \iota \to$ [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N k ∅`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}(S_k)$ generated by those generators, linear independence of $T$ over $\mathbb{Z}$ implies linear independence over $\mathbb{C}$ of the associated family of $\mathbb{C}$-linear endomorphisms of $S_k$.
--
--   This is the abstract form of the statement that the integral Hecke algebra of $S_k(\Gamma_0(N))$ remains independent after tensoring with $\mathbb{C}$, i.e. that $\mathbb{T}_{\mathbb{Z}} \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}} \mathbb{C} \to \operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}(S_k)$ is injective, with the properties of the Eichler–Shimura period map used in the classical rationality argument isolated as hypotheses (a)–(d). It is used by [`CuspForm.hasIntegralStructure_of_two_le`](thm.html#CuspForm.hasIntegralStructure_of_two_le) to equip spaces of cusp forms with an integral Hecke structure.
